"Random Scatter and Bias in a Measurement of g",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (accuracy and precision) and §{#} (the pendulum). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Hand timing, one swing; Hand timing, {#} swings; Photogate; A range of lengths; Length read to the bottom of the bob; Big swings, {#}°. Each setup sets the timer, the swings per timing, the length, the release angle and where the length is read; it does not clear your readings.",
"The Set up cards under the picture hold the <strong>Length L, as read on the meter stick</strong> (slider, the L −{#} cm, L +{#} cm and L +{#} cm buttons, or click its readout and type), the <strong>Release angle θ₀</strong>, the <strong>Timer</strong> (<strong>Hand stopwatch</strong> or <strong>Photogate</strong>), the <strong>Swings per timing N</strong> ({#}, {#}, {#} or {#}) and where the length is read (<strong>Center of the bob</strong> or <strong>Bottom of the bob</strong>). You can also drag the bob: sideways for θ₀, along the string for L.",
"<strong>Release</strong> swings the pendulum and times N swings, starting as the bob first passes the bottom; <strong>{#}×</strong> in the strip runs it faster. <strong>Repeat {#} times</strong> makes {#} more timings of the same setup at once. Every timing is recorded as a row in Readings by itself; × deletes a row.",
"The graph shows <strong>Repeated timings</strong> (a dot for each timing at the present setup, the mean, ± s and ± s/√n) or <strong>T² against L</strong> (every reading, the fitted line and the residuals below). The Analysis section gives the mean, s, s/√n and the fit’s slope and intercept. Blind (under Lab) hides g and the predicted periods.",
"The pendulum is exact: a point bob on a light string, no drag, g = {#} m/s², with the period at any release angle \\(T = T_0\\,\\frac{2}{\\pi}K\\!\\left(\\sin\\frac{\\theta_0}{2}\\right)\\), where \\(T_0 = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}\\).",
"The instrument errors are simulated, and labeled so. Each press of the hand stopwatch is early or late by a random amount with a standard deviation of {#} s; the display reads to {#} s. The photogate has no reaction time and reads to {#} s. Read to the bottom of the bob (diameter {#} cm), every length reading is {#} cm longer than the true length from pivot to bob center.",
"The random draws repeat when the page is opened again (same steps, same numbers); <strong>New random draws</strong> under Lab moves to another set, so your numbers can differ from a classmate’s.",
"Tell random scatter from bias in a measurement of g, decide which changes to the procedure make the result better, and report g with an uncertainty that the data justify.",
"A measurement has two kinds of error. <strong>Random error</strong> changes from one try to the next: a hand on a stopwatch is sometimes early and sometimes late. It shows up as scatter, and averaging many tries reduces it. <strong>Systematic error (bias)</strong> is the same on every try: a length read from the wrong point, or a formula that does not quite apply. It produces no scatter at all, so repeating never reveals it and never reduces it. A result can be <em>precise</em> (little scatter) and still <em>inaccurate</em> (biased).",
"For \\(n\\) repeated readings \\(T_1, \\dots, T_n\\) the mean is \\(\\bar T = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum T_i\\) and the standard deviation is",
"\\[s = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum (T_i - \\bar T)^2}{n-1}}.\\]",
"\\(s\\) describes one reading: about two thirds of single readings fall within \\(\\pm s\\) of the mean. The uncertainty of the <em>mean</em> is the standard error \\(s/\\sqrt{n}\\), which shrinks as you take more readings. An instrument also has a resolution: when every reading is identical, use half of its last digit as the uncertainty.",
"For a small swing, \\(T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}\\), so",
"\\[g = \\frac{4\\pi^2 L}{T^2},\\qquad \\frac{\\delta g}{g} = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\delta L}{L}\\right)^2 + \\left(2\\,\\frac{\\delta T}{T}\\right)^2}.\\]",
"The period counts twice because it is squared. Timing N swings at once divides the timing error by N: the start and stop presses are just as uncertain, but their error is now shared by N periods.",
"Squaring the period gives a straight line through the origin, \\(T^2 = (4\\pi^2/g)\\,L\\). Fitting \\(T^2\\) against \\(L\\) with a free intercept uses every length at once: the slope \\(k\\) gives \\(g = 4\\pi^2/k\\), and the intercept tests the model. If every length reading is too long by the same amount \\(d\\), the data follow \\(T^2 = (4\\pi^2/g)(L - d)\\): the slope is unchanged, and the line reaches \\(T^2 = 0\\) at \\(L = d\\) instead of at zero. Residuals (data minus line) should scatter evenly about zero; a pattern in them means the model or the procedure is wrong.",
"Finally, \\(2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}\\) is a small angle approximation. At larger angles the true period is longer, by about \\(\\theta_0^2/16\\) (in radians), and a g computed from the small angle formula comes out too low.",
"You time one swing of a pendulum ten times by hand and get readings that differ by up to {#} s. Is the mean of the ten better than any one of them? Why?",
"Which has the smaller uncertainty in the period: one swing timed ten times, or ten swings timed once? Predict before you measure.",
"A student reads every length to the bottom of the bob instead of its center. Will repeating the timings reveal the mistake? Will the slope of \\(T^2\\) against \\(L\\) be wrong? What about the intercept?",
"On a real bench, clamp the pivot firmly, keep the swing clear of people and equipment, and release the bob without a push.",
"Open <strong>Lab</strong> in the sidebar and check <strong>Blind: hide g and the predicted periods, to work them out yourself</strong>. Leave <strong>Reveal what the simulation hid</strong> unchecked until Part E. Every timing goes into Readings by itself. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> at the end of each part, then <strong>Clear</strong>, so each part gets its own copy of the Data table. Record each copy’s mean, s and s/√n from the Analysis section in the Summary table.",
